    {
      "step_id": "tile_python_comprehensions_iterating.s04",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "python.iterating_collections"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Iterating collections",
      "title": "Looping over any collection: lists, dicts, sets and tuples",
      "lede": "The same for loop syntax works on every collection — what changes is what you get back.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "block_id": "tile_python_comprehensions_iterating.s04.h1",
          "text": "Looping over any collection: lists, dicts, sets and tuples",
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_python_comprehensions_iterating.s04.p1",
          "text": "You've already looped over lists with for. The same for x in ... pattern works on other collections, but what you get back per pass differs. Looping directly over a dictionary yields its keys — for order_id in order_city: visits each order id, not each city. To get both key and value together, use .items(): for order_id, city in order_city.items(): print(order_id, city).",
          "role": "mechanism",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "python.iterating_collections"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "python.iterating_collections#f1",
              "python.iterating_collections#f2"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "python.iterating_collections#tb1",
              "python.iterating_collections#tb2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_python_comprehensions_iterating.s04.p2",
          "text": "A set's elements may come out in a different order than expected when iterated over, because a set is unordered — looping over a set of cities won't necessarily visit them in the order they were added. Tuples are a sequence type, so a for loop visits their items in order the same way it does for a list — for value in order: visits 1042, then 'Lagos', then 32, when order = (1042, 'Lagos', 32).",
          "role": "misconception",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "python.iterating_collections"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "python.iterating_collections#f3",
              "python.iterating_collections#f4"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "python.iterating_collections#tb3",
              "python.iterating_collections#tb4"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "side_by_side",
          "block_id": "tile_python_comprehensions_iterating.s04.sbs1",
          "left_label": "Collection type and loop header",
          "right_label": "What the loop variable holds each pass",
          "left": {
            "type": "code_walkthrough",
            "block_id": "tile_python_comprehensions_iterating.s04.sbs1.left",
            "language": "python",
            "code": "# list\nfor minutes in delivery_times: ...\n\n# dict (keys only)\nfor order_id in order_city: ...\n\n# dict (key + value)\nfor order_id, city in order_city.items(): ...\n\n# set\nfor city in {'Lagos', 'Abuja', 'Kano'}: ...\n\n# tuple\nfor value in (1042, 'Lagos', 32): ...",
            "reveal": "all"
          },
          "right": {
            "type": "code_walkthrough",
            "block_id": "tile_python_comprehensions_iterating.s04.sbs1.right",
            "language": "python",
            "code": "# each integer delivery time in order\n# e.g. 32, 45, 21, 60, 18 …\n\n# each integer key\n# e.g. 1042, 1043, 1044 …\n\n# each (key, value) pair\n# e.g. (1042, 'Lagos'), (1043, 'Abuja') …\n\n# each element — order not guaranteed\n# may be 'Kano', 'Lagos', 'Abuja' or any order\n\n# each item in sequence order\n# 1042, then 'Lagos', then 32",
            "reveal": "all"
          },
          "takeaway": "The for x in ... syntax is the same for every collection; what changes is what each pass hands you.",
          "lineage": {
